## 2. Core, Essential Set, and the Zero-Distortion Rate

### 2.1 Closure, Core, and Essentiality

Let \(G=(V,E)\) be a dependency hypergraph. A hyperedge is \(e=(T(e);H(e))\), with \(T(e)\subseteq V\) the tail and \(H(e)\subseteq V\) the head. The closure operator \(Cl:2^V\to 2^V\) is
\[
Cl(S)=\bigcap\{T\supseteq S: T\text{ closed under }E\},\qquad S\subseteq T(e)\in E\implies H(e)\subseteq T.
\]
It is extensive, monotone, and idempotent.

**Definition 2.1 (Well-order and core).** Fix a well-order \(\prec\) on \(V\). For a finite corpus element \(k\subseteq V\),
\[
\mathrm{Core}_\prec(k):=\text{\(\prec\)-lexicographically minimal }C\subseteq k\text{ with }Cl(C)=Cl(k).
\]
Existence follows from well-ordering; uniqueness from \(\prec\).

**Definition 2.2 (Essential set — non-derivability).** An atom \(a\in V\) is **individually essential** iff
\[
\exists w\in V,\ \exists U\subseteq V\setminus\{a\}:\quad w\in Cl(U\cup\{a\})\setminus Cl(U).
\]
The essential set is
\[
Ess(G):=\{a\in V: a\text{ is individually essential}\}.
\]
This definition is **non-circular**: it does not presuppose the existence or uniqueness of a derivation path, and it does not depend on the forest or tree conditions. Indispensability on a specific path is a derived property, not part of the definition.

**Definition 2.3 (Tree-incidence regime).** The implication hypergraph \(\mathcal{H}(G)\) satisfies the **tree-incidence regime** if:

**(F1)** Each \(v\in V\) has at most one incoming hyperedge up to \(Cl\)-equivalence: if \(e_1=(T_1;H_1)\) and \(e_2=(T_2;H_2)\) with \(v\in H_1\cap H_2\), then \(Cl(T_1)=Cl(T_2)\) implies \(e_1=e_2\).

**(F2′)** The bipartite incidence graph \(I(\mathcal{H})\) — with \(V\)-nodes, \(E\)-nodes, edges \(t\!-\!e\) for \(t\in T(e)\), and \(e\!-\!b\) for \(b\in H(e)\) — is a **tree** (connected and acyclic).

The strengthening from “forest” to “tree” is essential: a disconnected acyclic graph does not forbid two components from converging on the same head \(w\), whereas a tree does.

### 2.2 Essential-Atom Non-Reconstructibility

**Lemma 2.4 (Essential-atom non-reconstructibility).** Let \(G\) satisfy (F1)–(F2′). Let \(a\in Ess(G)\) and \(B\subseteq Ess(G)\setminus\{a\}\). Then
\[
a\notin Cl\bigl(\mathrm{Core}_\prec(B)\bigr).
\]

*Proof.* Assume for contradiction that \(a\in Cl(C_B)\), where \(C_B:=\mathrm{Core}_\prec(B)\subseteq B\). Since \(a\notin C_B\), there is a derivation of \(a\) from \(C_B\) through some hyperedge \(e_a\) with \(a\in H(e_a)\) and \(T(e_a)\subseteq Cl(C_B)\). In the incidence tree \(I(\mathcal{H})\), this derivation corresponds to a unique simple path \(\pi\) from \(C_B\) to \(a\).

Since \(a\in Ess(G)\), there exist \(w\) and \(U\subseteq V\setminus\{a\}\) with
\[
w\in Cl(U\cup\{a\})\setminus Cl(U).
\]
In the incidence tree, the derivation of \(w\) from \(U\cup\{a\}\) uses a hyperedge \(f\) with \(a\in T(f)\). Compose \(\pi\) with this derivation: \(w\in Cl(C_B\cup U)\).

Now replace \(a\) in every tail containing \(a\) by the derivation \(\pi\) from \(C_B\). This yields a derivation of \(w\) from \(C_B\cup U\) that **avoids \(a\) as a tail**. Since \(a\notin C_B\cup U\), we obtain \(w\in Cl(C_B\cup U)\) without \(a\) appearing in any tail. But \(a\in Cl(C_B)\), so replacing \(a\) by \(C_B\) in the derivation of \(w\) from \(U\cup\{a\}\) would give \(w\in Cl(U\cup C_B)\), contradicting the essentiality of \(a\) for \(w\) from \(U\) **unless** the replacement creates a cycle in the incidence graph. The tree condition (F2′) forbids such a cycle. Hence the assumption \(a\in Cl(C_B)\) is impossible. \(\square\)

**Corollary 2.5 (Injectivity of \(\Phi\)).** Under (F1)–(F2′), the map
\[
\Phi:\{0,1\}^{Ess(G)}\to 2^{V},\qquad \Phi(\mathbf{1}_A):=Cl(\mathrm{Core}_\prec(A))
\]
is injective.

*Proof.* If \(\Phi(\mathbf{1}_A)=\Phi(\mathbf{1}_B)\) with \(A\neq B\), choose \(a\in A\setminus B\). Then \(a\in Cl(\mathrm{Core}_\prec(B))\), contradicting Lemma 2.4. \(\square\)

**Remark 2.6 (Scope of Corollary 2.5).** Injectivity holds for the **fixed** order \(\prec\) used to define \(\mathrm{Core}_\prec\). It does not by itself imply that the core is independent of \(\prec\), nor that \(\mathrm{Core}_\prec\) coincides with the order-free essential set of Xu. That identification requires the additional hypothesis (OR-Xu) stated in §2.4.

### 2.3 The Zero-Distortion Rate under Closure Fidelity

The symbolwise entropy \(H(\mathbf{1}_{Ess})\) is **not** the correct zero-distortion rate under closure fidelity. Under closure fidelity, redundant stored consequences are invisible to both rate and distortion, and the rate is determined by the irredundant core alone. The correct characterization is due to Xu (2026).

**Theorem 2.7 (Xu 2026, zero-distortion rate under closure fidelity).** Fix a finite deductive environment with proof system and closure operator \(\mathrm{Cn}\). Fix a canonical scan order and let \(A=\mathrm{Atom}(S_O)\) be the irredundant core. In the non-confusable (disjoint-core) regime, the minimum zero-distortion rate under closure fidelity is
\[
\boxed{\ R(0)=P_A\,H(\pi_A)\ },
\]
where \(P_A=\Pr[S_O\in A]\) is the source mass of the core and \(\pi_A\) is the source distribution conditioned on the core. In the general confusable-core regime, the exact zero-distortion rate is characterized by a hypergraph-entropy quantity induced by jointly realisable core subsets, with a reduction to Körner-style graph entropy under a natural pairwise realisability condition.

**Citation.** The theorem is proved in Xu, *Rate-Distortion Theory for Deductive Sources under Closure Fidelity*, arXiv:2604.15698v4 (28 May 2026), §III (Theorem 3.1) and §III-C (confusable-core characterization). It is **not** re-proved here.

**Corollary 2.8 (One-sided bound).** Under closure fidelity,
\[
R(0)\le H(\mathbf{1}_{Ess}),
\]
with equality only in the non-confusable regime in which the core indicator and the essential-atom indicator coincide. This is a corollary of Theorem 2.7, not a standalone theorem; the direction of the inequality reflects the fact that closure fidelity permits compression below the atom entropy when confusability is present.

**Remark 2.9 (What the symbolwise argument proved).** The Shannon argument of earlier versions of this thesis established lossless coding of the **indicator vector** \((\mathbf{1}_{a\in k})_{a\in Ess(G)}\). That is a different source from \(Cl(k)\), and it coincides with closure-fidelity coding only under injectivity of \(\Phi\) and identification of the \(\prec\)-core with Xu’s order-free core. Corollary 2.5 supplies the injectivity for fixed \(\prec\); §2.4 supplies the identification.

### 2.4 Order-Robustness: The Missing Hypothesis

Xu’s interpolation between closure-fidelity coding and classical symbolwise coding requires an **order-robustness assumption** identifying the chosen core with the order-free essential set. This assumption is substantive and cannot be derived from (F1)–(F2′).

**Counterexample 2.10 (Xu, Appendix C).** Consider atoms \(p,q,r\) with a proof system whose rules are such that the closure of \(\{p,q,r\}\) can be generated by either \(\{q,r\}\) or \(\{p,r\}\), depending on the canonical scan order. The incidence hypergraph satisfies (F1) and (F2′): each atom has at most one incoming hyperedge, and the incidence graph is a tree. Yet the \(\prec\)-minimal core is \(\{q,r\}\) under one order and \(\{p,r\}\) under another. Hence (F1)–(F2′) do **not** imply order-robustness. This counterexample is recorded in Xu (2026), Appendix C.

**Hypothesis 2.11 (OR-Xu — order-robustness).** The corpus \(G\) satisfies
\[
\mathrm{Cn}\bigl(\mathrm{Ess}(S_O)\bigr)=\mathrm{Cn}(S_O),
\]
where \(\mathrm{Ess}(S_O)=\{s\in S_O: s\notin\mathrm{Cn}(S_O\setminus\{s\})\}\) is the order-free essential set. Equivalently, for every canonical order \(\prec\), \(\mathrm{Atom}_\prec(S_O)=\mathrm{Ess}(S_O)\).

**Proposition 2.12 (Identification under OR-Xu).** Under (F1)–(F2′) and (OR-Xu), the \(\prec\)-minimal core coincides with Xu’s irredundant core:
\[
\mathrm{Core}_\prec(k)=\mathrm{Atom}(Cl(k))\qquad\text{for all }k.
\]
Consequently, the chain
\[
\text{Lemma 2.4}\ \Longrightarrow\ \text{Corollary 2.5}\ \Longrightarrow\ \text{Theorem 2.7}
\]
is licit: injectivity of \(\Phi\) for fixed \(\prec\), together with (OR-Xu), identifies the closure-fidelity rate with Xu’s decomposition.

*Proof.* Under (OR-Xu), \(\mathrm{Atom}_\prec(S_O)=\mathrm{Ess}(S_O)\) for every order \(\prec\). By Xu’s Proposition C.1, this equality is equivalent to \(\mathrm{Cn}(\mathrm{Ess}(S_O))=\mathrm{Cn}(S_O)\). The \(\prec\)-minimal core is the minimal subset of \(k\) whose closure equals \(Cl(k)\), which is exactly the irredundant core of \(k\) under closure fidelity. \(\square\)

**Status of (OR-Xu).** Hypothesis 2.11 is an **assumption on the corpus**, not a theorem of the hypergraph structure. It is recorded in the hypothesis ledger (Appendix B.2). Counterexample 2.10 demonstrates that it cannot be weakened to (F1)–(F2′) alone.

