## 3. Confusable Blocks and Hypergraph Reachability

### 3.1 Directed Hypergraph Reachability

**Definition 3.1 (Implication hypergraph).** The implication hypergraph of \(G\) is
\[
\mathcal{H}(G):=(V,E),\qquad V:=Ess(G),\qquad (a_1,\dots,a_k;b)\in E \iff b\in Cl(\{a_1,\dots,a_k\})\setminus\bigcup_j Cl(\{a_j\}).
\]

**Definition 3.2 (Reachability — Allamigeon).** For \(u,v\in V\), \(v\) is reachable from \(u\), written \(u\rightsquigarrow v\), iff \(u=v\), or there exists a hyperedge \(e=(T(e);H(e))\in E\) with \(v\in H(e)\) such that every \(t\in T(e)\) satisfies \(u\rightsquigarrow t\). This is the recursive all-tail definition standard in directed hypergraph theory.

**Definition 3.3 (Confusable blocks).** Two atoms \(a,b\in Ess(G)\) are confusable if there exists a directed cycle through both under \(\rightsquigarrow\). The confusable blocks are the equivalence classes of the transitive closure of confusability. Strongly connected components are taken with respect to \(\rightsquigarrow\).

### 3.2 Strongly Connected Components: Terminal SCCs, Not Tarjan

**Proposition 3.4 (Allamigeon 2011/2014).** There is an algorithm computing the **terminal** strongly connected components of a directed hypergraph — i.e. SCCs which do not reach any components but themselves — in time
\[
O\bigl(\mathsf{size}(\mathcal{H})\,\alpha(n)\bigr),
\]
where \(\mathsf{size}(\mathcal{H})=|V|+\sum_{e\in E}(|T(e)|+|H(e)|)\), \(n=|V|\), and \(\alpha\) is the inverse Ackermann function.

**Proposition 3.5 (Hardness of all SCCs).** Computing **all** SCCs of a directed hypergraph is strictly harder: there is a superlinear lower bound on the size of the transitive reduction of the reachability relation, and a linear-time reduction from the problem of finding all minimal sets among a given family (for which only subquadratic algorithms are known).

**Algorithmic consequence.** Algorithm 4 (confusable-block decomposition) uses Allamigeon’s terminal-SCC algorithm. It does **not** use Tarjan or Kosaraju, and it does **not** claim quasi-linear computation of all SCCs. Terminal SCCs suffice for partitioning \(Ess(G)\) into confusable blocks, because each block is a maximal set of mutually reachable atoms, and terminal SCCs are exactly the maximal such sets under the reachability preorder.

**Remark 3.6 (Rate characterization in the confusable regime).** The confusable-block decomposition is a structural diagnostic. It is **not** a rate formula. Xu’s Theorem 2.7 characterizes the confusable-core rate via a hypergraph-entropy quantity, not via a sum of local block entropies. Earlier versions of this thesis proposed such a sum as a conjecture; that conjecture is superseded by Xu’s characterization and is not retained.

