# Part II — Architecture

## 5. The FEL

### 5.1 LWW Join under a Total Order

**Definition 5.1 (LWW entry).** An LWW-Map entry is a triple \(\ell=(\text{target},t,r)\), with \(t\in\mathbb{N}\) an HLC scalar and \(r\in\mathrm{ReplicaID}\).

**Definition 5.2 (LWW order).** Define
\[
\ell_1\preceq\ell_2 \iff (t_1<t_2)\ \text{or}\ (t_1=t_2\ \text{and}\ r_1\le_{\mathrm{lex}}r_2),
\]
with \(\le_{\mathrm{lex}}\) the lexicographic order on replica IDs.

**Lemma 5.3 (Totality).** \(\preceq\) is a total order on entries of the same key.

*Proof.* For any two entries, either \(t_1<t_2\), \(t_2<t_1\), or \(t_1=t_2\); in the last case, lexicographic order on replica IDs is total. \(\square\)

**Rule 5.4 (LWW-Map join).** \((E\cup_{\mathrm{LWW}}E')(e):=\max_{\preceq}(E(e),E'(e))\).

**Theorem 5.5 (Join-semilattice).** Under Lemma 5.3, the LWW-Map join is commutative, associative, and idempotent. Componentwise join with OR-Set union yields a join-semilattice.

*Proof.* Max under a total order is commutative, associative, and idempotent. OR-Set union is the standard join on tag pairs. Componentwise join of join-semilattices is a join-semilattice. \(\square\)

**Remark 5.6 (Stamp uniqueness demoted).** Invariant 5.2 of earlier versions (stamp uniqueness) is **stronger than necessary** for Theorem 5.5. Lexicographic tie-breaking on replica IDs already gives totality, hence a join-semilattice. Stamp uniqueness is retained as an **implementation optimization** for HLC collision avoidance, not as a hypothesis of the algebra. This matches the standard LWW-register construction, in which the state is ordered by \((t,r)\) lexicographically.

### 5.2 Hybrid OR-Set + RW-Set State

The FEL requires both add-wins concurrency for ordinary knowledge nodes and remove-wins semantics for epistemic retraction. A single OR-Set cannot provide both. The state is therefore a product:

**Definition 5.7 (FEL state).**
\[
\mathrm{FEL} := (OR\text{-}Set_{add},\ RW\text{-}Set_{epistemic}).
\]
The \(OR\)-Set is add-wins: a concurrent add and remove leave the element present if the add was not observed by the remove. The \(RW\)-Set is remove-wins: a concurrent add and remove leave the element absent. Both are join-semilattices under their respective joins.

**Proposition 5.8 (Product semilattice).** The componentwise join on \(\mathrm{FEL}\) is a join-semilattice.

*Proof.* The product of two join-semilattices is a join-semilattice under componentwise join. \(\square\)

**Remark 5.9 (Design choice, not theorem).** The allocation of nodes to the \(OR\) component versus the \(RW\) component is an **application-level design decision**. It is not a theorem of CRDT algebra. The thesis recommends \(RW\) for nodes whose retraction is intended to be permanent (epistemic integrity) and \(OR\) for ordinary collaborative knowledge where concurrent additions should survive. This hybrid design is consistent with the literature on combining add-wins and remove-wins policies.

### 5.3 Delivery and Convergence

**Hypothesis (RD).** Every delta produced at a replica is eventually delivered to every other replica at least once. Duplicate delivery is absorbed by idempotence. No delta is permanently lost.

**Corollary 5.10 (Strong eventual consistency).** Under (RD) and Proposition 5.8, any two replicas that have received the same set of deltas have identical state.

*Proof.* Merge is idempotent, commutative, and associative by Proposition 5.8. Under (RD), every delta reaches every replica; the state is the join of all received deltas. Equal delta sets give equal joins. \(\square\)

### 5.4 Garbage Collection

**Definition 5.11 (Causal stability).** A tombstone for node \(n\) is causally stable at replica \(i\) if every replica has acknowledged receipt of the removal delta for \(n\).

**Proposition 5.12 (Stale-delta safety for the OR component).** Under (RD), GC of an OR-Set tombstone for tag \(t_1\) after \(t_1\) is causally stable cannot be resurrected by a late delivery of a duplicate delta carrying \(t_1\).

*Proof.* Any delta carrying \(t_1\) reaches every replica by (RD). If it arrives before the removal, it is removed at the replica performing the removal; if it arrives after, it is merged into a state where \(t_1\) is tombstoned and is idempotently absorbed. \(\square\)

**Proposition 5.13 (Remove-wins generation suppression).** Suppose \(RW\)-Set nodes carry a generation index \(g\), and removal increments a deletion generation \(g_{\mathrm{del}}\) that participates in the causal-stability protocol. Then an add with generation \(g\le g_{\mathrm{del}}\) is suppressed by any replica that has merged the removal. Under (RD) and the generation invariant, GC of a stable generation-\(g\) tombstone is safe against any add of generation \(\le g\).

*Proof.* The generation invariant ensures that an add with \(g\le g_{\mathrm{del}}\) is dominated in the \(RW\)-Set join by the removal of generation \(g_{\mathrm{del}}\). Under (RD) the removal reaches every replica; idempotence ensures that late arrival of the dominated add does not alter the state. \(\square\)

**Remark 5.14 (Scope).** Proposition 5.12 concerns duplicate late delivery of an already-known tag in the \(OR\) component. Proposition 5.13 concerns the \(RW\) component with generations. Neither proposition suppresses a genuinely new add with a fresh tag in a pure OR-Set; such an add is handled by the add-wins semantics of the \(OR\) component, which is intentional.

