## 6. Controversy

### 6.1 The Stance Lattice

**Definition 6.1 (Stance lattice).** Let
\[
\mathcal{S}:=\{0<\text{challenged}<\text{supported}<\text{resolved}\}
\]
with join \(=\max\) and meet \(=\min\). The lattice is distributive and finite.

**Definition 6.2 (Position).** A position is a pair \((s,\delta)\), where \(s\in\mathcal{S}\) and \(\delta\subseteq E_{\mathrm{controversy}}\) is a derivation (a set of hyperedges in the controversy hypergraph).

**Definition 6.3 (Supersession).** A supersession delta is an explicit delta \(\delta_{\mathrm{sup}}\) with \(s\ge\mathrm{resolved}\). A position derived from a descendant of a resolution may not lower the stance below \(\mathrm{resolved}\) without a supersession delta.

### 6.2 Laundering Prevention

**Rule 6.4.** A controversy node that has been resolved cannot be re-opened by a position that derives from a descendant of the resolution without an explicit supersession delta.

**Theorem 6.5 (Laundering prevention).** Under Rule 6.4 and the stance-lattice algebra, no position can launder a resolved controversy into an unresolved one by composition of derivation and position updates.

*Proof.* The proof is by induction on derivation depth. The base case is a leaf position: its stance is either below \(\mathrm{resolved}\) (in which case it is not a descendant of the resolution) or at least \(\mathrm{resolved}\) (in which case it is already resolved). For the inductive step, the stance of a composed position is the join of the stances of its immediate predecessors. Since join is monotone and the lattice is finite, the composed stance is bounded above by the join of the leaf stances. If no supersession delta is present, Rule 6.4 forbids any descendant of the resolution from contributing a stance below \(\mathrm{resolved}\). Hence the join remains at least \(\mathrm{resolved}\). No composition can lower it. \(\square\)

**Remark 6.6 (Why a concrete lattice is necessary).** The theorem is provable because \(\mathcal{S}\) is finite and the join is monotone. For an unspecified lattice, the monotonicity argument does not go through, and the theorem is not well-posed. The four-element lattice of Definition 6.1 is the minimal concrete model in which the laundering-prevention claim has content.

