# Part IV — Experimental Protocol

The protocol is specified as follows.

**(E1) Datasets.** A knowledge base with a proof system (e.g. a Datalog fragment with materialized views, or a curated mathematical corpus). Report \(|V|\), \(|E|\), maximum hyperedge arity, and the fraction of atoms in non-singleton terminal SCCs.

**(E2) Rate measurement.** Compute \(P_{\mathrm{core}}\) and \(H(\pi_A)\) empirically under a fixed canonical order. Compare against the symbolwise entropy \(H(\mathbf{1}_{Ess})\). Report the gap as a function of confusability.

**(E3) Order-robustness.** For each corpus, test whether \(\mathrm{Atom}_\prec(S_O)=\mathrm{Ess}(S_O)\) for at least two distinct canonical orders. If the equality fails, report the corpus as **not** satisfying (OR-Xu), and interpret rate measurements accordingly.

**(E4) SCC correctness.** Verify Allamigeon’s terminal-SCC algorithm against brute-force reachability on small hypergraphs. Report agreement and runtime.

**(E5) GC safety.** Simulate at-least-once delivery with delays. Measure resurrection events under (i) OR-Set with causal stability only, (ii) RW-Set with generations, and (iii) the hybrid state. Report resurrection rate and tombstone lifetime.

**(E6) Controversy.** Construct synthetic controversies with adversarial derivation sequences. Measure whether any sequence lowers a resolved stance without a supersession delta. Report 0/1.

Each experimental claim is tagged with the hypothesis set under which it is interpreted. Empirical success does not promote a conditional theorem to an unconditional one.

